# SEQUENCE 14 - WHAT WAS DUE

EXT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - FRONT VERANDA - EARLY AFTERNOON

The market bags arrive before anybody explains the faces carrying them.

Bode sets tomatoes, pepper and fish on the veranda table.

Eno looks at the bags.

Then at Kai's hand.

The tremor is back.

Ade still has one thumb pressed below his ear.

Malik is breathing normally.

He does not trust that yet.

ENO
You found pepper.

BODE
Yes.

ENO
What else?

Nobody answers.

Eno looks at Bode.

He tries to hold her gaze.

Cannot.

Kai does it for him.

KAI
It happened there.

ENO
At the market.

Kai nods.

Eno looks across the courtyard.

The compound gate.

The front room.

The kitchen.

The mouth of the back passage.

All ordinary in daylight.

ENO
Yesterday the house.

She looks back at them.

ENO
Today the market.

ADE
We're covering ground.

The joke lands nowhere.

Ade lets it die.

Eno studies them another moment.

ENO
Before all this, you said something was late.

Malik looks at her.

MALIK
Nine days.

ENO
What was it?

MALIK
We don't know.

ENO
Then find that first.

BODE
Eno --

ENO
I did not say fix anything.

To the brothers --

ENO
Find out what was late.

Malik looks toward the back passage.

Then to Kai.

Then Ade.

He waits.

MALIK
Room only.

KAI
Room only.

Ade nods.

ADE
Together.

Bode picks up the household keys.

CUT TO:

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - BACK PASSAGE - MOMENTS LATER

Daylight reaches halfway down the passage.

The rest waits in cool shadow.

At the low end, Bode works the simple hitch in the outer RED CORD open by hand.

The brothers stand behind him.

Nobody crowds the door.

Bode unlocks it.

The four stone steps descend into the maintenance room.

Kai's right hand twitches once at his side.

Malik sees it.

MALIK
If one of us wants out, we go.

Kai looks at him.

KAI
One of us.

MALIK
One of us.

Ade checks both of them.

ADE
Good.

They go down together.

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - MAINTENANCE ROOM - CONTINUOUS

Daylight through the high slats.

Camphor.

Paper.

Machine oil.

Nothing waits for them.

The INNER DOOR remains closed.

The field of red cord around it hangs still.

But it is not where it was.

Two knots sit below pencil marks that once met them exactly.

One loose tail rests at a different angle from the rows beside it.

Malik stops.

MALIK
That wasn't below the mark.

Kai looks only long enough to understand what Malik means.

KAI
No.

ADE
Nobody came down here last night.

BODE
No.

Malik takes one step closer.

His hand rises toward the lower knot.

Stops.

He lowers it.

MALIK
Nobody touches anything.

KAI
I wasn't going to.

Malik looks at his own hand.

MALIK
Neither was I.

He backs away from the door.

That costs him something.

They move to the worktable.

Malik opens Solomon's ledger to the maintenance schedule.

The missed date waits where they left it.

Nine days late.

Kai stays on the far side of the table, his body angled away from the inner door.

Ade takes the chair that lets him see both brothers and the stone steps.

Bode remains beside the cabinet.

Malik turns to the ruled intervals before the missed one.

One completed date.

He follows its reference mark deeper into the ledger.

A page of observations.

CHECKED.

MEASURED.

BRASS -- CLEAN.

RIVER +11 CM.

Nothing else.

Two pages later --

WEST BINDING -- REPLACED.

RE-MEASURED.

Malik goes back to the schedule.

Another completed interval.

Another reference mark.

CHECKED.

MEASURED.

NO CHANGE.

No correction follows it.

Kai watches the pattern form.

Malik checks a third interval.

Again --

observation first.

Correction only when something had changed.

Kai looks at the missed date.

KAI
The date told him when to look.

Malik keeps reading.

MALIK
Not what he'd find.

Ade looks at the nine-day gap.

ADE
So we're nine days late looking.

Nobody likes the sentence.

Malik turns another page.

The same structure.

Inspection.

Finding.

Then, sometimes, work.

MALIK
This wasn't a repair date.

KAI
It was a check.

Malik nods.

For the first time, the delay becomes something they can understand without pretending they understand the system.

MALIK
We can repeat the inspection.

KAI
Without repeating the repair.

MALIK
We don't know the repair.

ADE
Even better reason not to improvise one.

Kai moves closer to the table.

MALIK
I can record what changed.

He looks at Kai.

The next part is harder.

MALIK
If the place starts changing while we're looking -- you say it.

KAI
And if I say stop?

MALIK
We stop.

ADE
Any one of us says stop, we stop.

Malik nods.

Kai does too.

Bode watches the three brothers settle the rule without him.

He stays out of it.

Malik turns back to the index.

BINDING MATERIALS.

BRASS.

WESTERN STONES.

RIVER LEVEL.

INNER FRAME.

He takes the back of the market receipt.

Writes only what prior inspection pages clearly measure without changing anything:

WESTERN STONES.

RIVER LEVEL.

INNER FRAME.

He looks again at BRASS.

Writes it beneath the others.

Adds a question mark.

KAI
The talisman.

Malik looks toward the ceiling -- toward the house above them, where the removed talisman remains under ordinary cloth.

Then at the brass markers, anchor points and fittings around the room.

MALIK
Maybe.

He taps the word BRASS.

MALIK
Or any of this.

Ade looks around at how much brass Solomon worked with.

ADE
Then we don't turn one word into an instruction.

Malik draws a line beneath BRASS ?

MALIK
Unknown. Untouched.

KAI
Agreed.

Malik looks at BINDING MATERIALS.

Does not add it to the inspection list.

MALIK
Materials are work. We don't know what needs work yet.

Ade nods toward the three measurable headings.

ADE
Measure. Don't repair.

BODE
And the western stones?

Kai's jaw tightens.

The western stones are where he left the maintained path.

MALIK
We stay on the path.

KAI
And if the path isn't where it was?

Nobody answers immediately.

Ade reaches over.

Taps the receipt once.

ADE
Then that's where we stop.

Malik nods.

Kai nods.

Bode does too.

At the top of the stone steps --

Eno.

She has not entered the room.

ENO
Well?

Malik turns toward her.

MALIK
The date was an inspection.

ENO
Can you do it?

Malik looks at Kai.

Kai looks at Ade.

Ade looks at Bode.

Only then does Malik answer.

MALIK
We can check what changed.

ENO
Today?

Malik hesitates.

That is answer enough.

ENO
No.

MALIK
Every day makes it later.

Eno looks at the dried blood still caught at the edge of Ade's nostril.

Kai's shaking hand.

Malik's too-careful breathing.

ENO
Tomorrow will still be late.

She steps back from the doorway.

ENO
It will also be daylight.

No one moves.

ENO
Today you eat.

A beat.

ENO
Tomorrow, if you are still going, you go together.

Nobody argues.

Malik closes Solomon's ledger.

Kai leaves his aunt's notebook where it is.

Ade takes the market receipt with the inspection list.

Bode turns toward the stairs.

Kai goes first.

He does not look back.

Ade follows.

Bode after him.

Malik is last.

At the bottom step, he looks once at the two lowered knots.

Does not go back to correct them.

He climbs into the daylight.

Behind him --

The inner door remains closed.

The displaced cord remains exactly as they found it.

CUT TO BLACK.
